<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  ObliWAN Bench Tool — Windows MSI installer.

  Prerequisites (one-time, on the build host):
    dotnet tool install -g wix

  Build by hand, from server/ (the release script does the same in a staging
  directory, verifies both signatures, and only then publishes into
  dist-bench — see build.mjs):
    node src/bench/build.mjs                                 -> dist-bench/obliwan-bench.exe
    powershell -ExecutionPolicy Bypass -Command "& '<signing script>' -Targets 'dist-bench\obliwan-bench.exe'"
    wix build dist-bench/product.wxs -b dist-bench -arch x64 -out dist-bench/obliwan-bench.msi
    powershell -ExecutionPolicy Bypass -Command "& '<signing script>' -Targets 'dist-bench\obliwan-bench.msi'"
  Doing it by hand REPLACES what dist-bench publishes: build.mjs run
  without its out option empties it first (the option is not spelled out
  here: XML forbids a double hyphen inside a comment, and wix refuses the
  whole file over one). From then until dist-bench is whole again (exe AND
  MSI both validly signed, VERSION equal to src/bench/VERSION), or emptied
  of all three on purpose, the release script refuses any server release
  that does not rebuild the bench, and refuses before bumping anything. A
  MISSING file is refused like an unsigned one: stopping after the exe
  leaves a signed exe and no MSI, an image built from that serves no
  installer, and every bench then stops being offered updates without
  anything saying so.

  It is dist-bench/product.wxs that wix builds, never this file: build.mjs
  writes that copy with the version placeholder of Package/@Version
  replaced, and the placeholder itself is not a version wix accepts.
  build.mjs replaces the FIRST occurrence only, so the token must appear in
  this file exactly once, on Package/@Version. Spelled out in a comment
  above it, it is the comment that receives the version, wix refuses the
  build (WIX0108), and every bench release aborts at its MSI step.

  THE ORDER IS THE POINT: the exe is signed BEFORE the MSI is built, so the
  binary embedded in the installer is itself signed; the MSI is then signed
  separately. Same sequence as Obliview's `:BUILD_AGENT_WIN`.

  ONE DELIBERATE DEVIATION FROM THE OTHER OBLI* PRODUCTS: there, a failed
  signature is logged and the pipeline continues. Here it must ABORT. This
  binary writes administrator accounts onto brand-new routers; an unsigned copy
  reaching a preparation workstation is exactly what the signature exists to
  prevent, and "Sign:ECHEC" in a summary nobody reads is not a control.

  ┌─ WHAT THIS INSTALLER DELIBERATELY DOES NOT DO ─────────────────────────────┐
  │ No service, no scheduled task, no autostart. The bench tool runs when a     │
  │ preparer runs it and not one second longer: it is the only component of     │
  │ ObliWAN allowed to write local accounts onto a device, and a thing with     │
  │ that permission must not be resident.                                       │
  │                                                                            │
  │ It DOES store its one setting, and that reversed an earlier decision.       │
  │ This comment used to say it stored nothing, on the reasoning that "a        │
  │ preparation workstation is a shared machine". That is an assertion about    │
  │ somebody's fleet, not a rule — and on this one it is false: the bench PC is │
  │ a hardened, single-purpose, access-controlled machine. Designing against a  │
  │ threat the deployment does not have bought nothing and cost a great deal:   │
  │ it made a preparer open a command prompt and set environment variables      │
  │ before every session, which is exactly the fiddling an installer exists to  │
  │ remove — and a step skipped under pressure is a step that stops happening.  │
  │                                                                            │
  │ So OBLIWANURL is an installer property, written to obliwan-bench.ini        │
  │ beside the executable, and remembered across upgrades (see the property    │
  │ below). The preparer double-clicks a shortcut and answers questions about  │
  │ the ROUTER, never about ObliWAN.                                            │
  │                                                                            │
  │ This comment once named an OBLIWANTOKEN property as well. None exists in   │
  │ this file: the installer writes no credential, only a URL. The .ini sits   │
  │ in Program Files and the remembered copy in HKLM — both readable by any    │
  │ local account, writable only by an administrator — and what they disclose  │
  │ is where the ObliWAN instance lives, which is not a secret.                │
  └────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
-->
<Wix xmlns="http://wixtoolset.org/schemas/v4/wxs">
  <Package Name="ObliWAN Bench Tool"
           Manufacturer="ObliWAN"
           Version="BENCH_VERSION_PLACEHOLDER"
           Language="1033"
           Scope="perMachine"
           UpgradeCode="8F2E4A61-3B7C-4D0E-9A15-6C2D8E4F1B03">

    <MajorUpgrade DowngradeErrorMessage="A newer version of the ObliWAN Bench Tool is already installed." />
    <MediaTemplate EmbedCab="yes" />

    <!--
      The setting, supplied at the FIRST install:

        msiexec /i obliwan-bench.msi OBLIWANURL=https://obliwan.example.com

      `Secure="yes"` lets it survive an elevated install (without it, MSI
      discards public properties passed from a non-elevated caller).

      ┌─ REMEMBERED, BECAUSE EVERY UPGRADE IS AN UNINSTALL FIRST ────────────┐
      │ Windows Installer keeps no public property from one install to the   │
      │ next, and MajorUpgrade removes the old product                       │
      │ (afterInstallValidate) before writing the new one — which deletes    │
      │ the old `url=` line. So an upgrade started with no OBLIWANURL (the   │
      │ tool's self-update, before it passed one; or a preparer              │
      │ double-clicking the MSI from "Download app") used to write `url=`    │
      │ EMPTY: every bench forgot its server on every update, and            │
      │ config.ts's "the MSI writes it ONCE, at install" held exactly once.  │
      │                                                                      │
      │ The fix is the standard remember-property pattern:                   │
      │   - BenchConfig also writes the value to HKLM\Software\ObliWAN\Bench │
      │     (`url`);                                                         │
      │   - AppSearch reads it back — it runs before RemoveExistingProducts, │
      │     so the OLD product's value is still there to read;               │
      │   - but AppSearch OVERWRITES a property given on the command line,   │
      │     so the command-line value is saved before AppSearch and put back │
      │     after it. A technician re-pointing a bench with                  │
      │     `OBLIWANURL=<new>` therefore wins over the remembered one.       │
      │ Both SetProperty actions run in BOTH sequences on purpose: AppSearch │
      │ runs in each, and in the execute sequence the "saved" value is the   │
      │ one the UI sequence settled on, so a second AppSearch cannot undo    │
      │ it either.                                                           │
      │                                                                      │
      │ Upgrading FROM a version without this registry value has nothing to  │
      │ read back, so it keeps the URL only if the upgrade passes OBLIWANURL │
      │ itself. The bench's self-update does, from the .ini it read          │
      │ (selfUpdate.ts, msiexecArguments); a double-click on the new MSI     │
      │ does not, and loses the URL that one time. (IniFileSearch cannot     │
      │ help — Windows Installer only looks for .ini files in the Windows    │
      │ directory.)                                                          │
      └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
    -->
    <Property Id="OBLIWANURL" Secure="yes">
      <RegistrySearch Id="RememberedObliwanUrl"
                      Root="HKLM"
                      Key="Software\ObliWAN\Bench"
                      Name="url"
                      Type="raw" />
    </Property>
    <SetProperty Id="CMDLINE_OBLIWANURL"
                 Action="SaveCmdlineObliwanUrl"
                 Value="[OBLIWANURL]"
                 Before="AppSearch" />
    <SetProperty Id="OBLIWANURL"
                 Action="RestoreCmdlineObliwanUrl"
                 Value="[CMDLINE_OBLIWANURL]"
                 After="AppSearch"
                 Condition="CMDLINE_OBLIWANURL" />

    <StandardDirectory Id="ProgramFiles64Folder">
      <Directory Id="INSTALLFOLDER" Name="ObliWAN Bench">
        <Component Id="BenchExe" Guid="1D6B9C34-7E20-4A8F-B5C1-92E07A3D4F68">
          <File Id="ObliwanBenchExe"
                Source="obliwan-bench.exe"
                Name="obliwan-bench.exe"
                KeyPath="yes" />
        </Component>

        <!--
          Written beside the executable, removed with it. `IniFile` rather than
          a registry key so the tool can read it with `fs.readFileSync` and no
          native module: this binary is a Node SEA, and adding a registry
          dependency to reach one string would be a build problem in exchange
          for nothing. The registry copy below is for the INSTALLER, which
          cannot read an .ini outside the Windows directory; the tool never
          reads it.

          A FIRST install with no OBLIWANURL writes an empty value, and the
          tool treats empty as absent and asks. An UPGRADE with no OBLIWANURL
          writes back the remembered one (see the property above).
        -->
        <Component Id="BenchConfig" Guid="7A4F1E92-0C86-4B3D-A71F-5E2D0B94C863">
          <IniFile Id="BenchUrl"
                   Action="addLine"
                   Directory="INSTALLFOLDER"
                   Name="obliwan-bench.ini"
                   Section="server"
                   Key="url"
                   Value="[OBLIWANURL]" />
          <!-- What RememberedObliwanUrl reads back on the next upgrade. -->
          <RegistryValue Root="HKLM"
                         Key="Software\ObliWAN\Bench"
                         Name="url"
                         Type="string"
                         Value="[OBLIWANURL]" />
          <RegistryValue Root="HKLM"
                         Key="Software\ObliWAN\Bench"
                         Name="configured"
                         Type="integer"
                         Value="1"
                         KeyPath="yes" />
        </Component>
      </Directory>
    </StandardDirectory>

    <!--
      A Start-menu entry only. No Run key, no service: see the box above.
      The target is the application itself: build.mjs marks obliwan-bench.exe
      as a Windows GUI-subsystem program, so the shortcut opens the
      application window (Edge in app mode) and no console. No argument is
      passed: the console mode (the cli switch) no longer exists.
    -->
    <StandardDirectory Id="ProgramMenuFolder">
      <Directory Id="AppShortcutFolder" Name="ObliWAN">
        <Component Id="BenchShortcut" Guid="4C81E7A2-95D3-46B8-8F70-B3A6D1902E5C">
          <Shortcut Id="BenchStartMenu"
                    Name="ObliWAN Bench Tool"
                    Description="Prepare a new router for ObliWAN"
                    Target="[INSTALLFOLDER]obliwan-bench.exe"
                    WorkingDirectory="INSTALLFOLDER" />
          <RemoveFolder Id="RemoveAppShortcutFolder" Directory="AppShortcutFolder" On="uninstall" />
          <RegistryValue Root="HKCU"
                         Key="Software\ObliWAN\Bench"
                         Name="installed"
                         Type="integer"
                         Value="1"
                         KeyPath="yes" />
        </Component>
      </Directory>
    </StandardDirectory>

    <Feature Id="Main" Title="ObliWAN Bench Tool" Level="1">
      <ComponentRef Id="BenchExe" />
      <ComponentRef Id="BenchShortcut" />
      <!-- Every Component needs a parent Feature or WiX refuses the build with
           WIX0267. Adding the Component without this line is exactly how a
           settings file silently stops being installed. -->
      <ComponentRef Id="BenchConfig" />
    </Feature>
  </Package>
</Wix>
